  <body>
    <main class="project-page">

      <!-- BACK -->
      <a class="back-link" href="/#projects">← Back to portfolio</a>


      <!-- HERO -->
      <header class="project-hero">

        <p class="project-label">
          Analog Circuits • Feedback Control • Spring 2026
        </p>

        <h1>Closed-Loop Motor Control System</h1>

        <p class="project-summary">
          Experimentally characterized DC motors, developed and validated a
          mathematical motor model, designed an encoder-based speed sensor, and
          built an op-amp feedback controller that achieved a critically damped
          motor-speed response.
        </p>

      </header>


      <!-- HERO / PHYSICAL SETUP IMAGE -->
      <div class="hero-image">
        <img
          src="/projects/motor-control/hero.jpeg"
          alt="Closed-loop DC motor control system physical setup"
        />
      </div>


      <!-- PROJECT META -->
      <section class="project-meta">

        <div>
          <span>Focus</span>
          <strong>Feedback Control</strong>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>Controller</span>
          <strong>Analog I-Compensator</strong>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>Feedback</span>
          <strong>Encoder Speed Sensor</strong>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>Result</span>
          <strong>Critically Damped</strong>
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- PROJECT GOAL -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">Project Goal</p>

        <h2>
          Control motor speed using measured feedback rather than fixed voltage.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The project began by experimentally characterizing the DC motors and
          developing a model of their electrical and mechanical behavior. That
          model was then used to design a closed-loop speed-control system.
        </p>

        <p>
          A reference voltage represented the desired motor speed. An
          encoder-based sensor measured the actual speed and converted it into
          a proportional voltage. The controller compared the desired and
          measured speeds and continuously adjusted the motor drive to reduce
          the error.
        </p>


        <!-- SIMPLE CONTROL LOOP -->
        <div class="control-flow">

          <div class="flow-block">
            <span>1</span>
            <strong>Reference</strong>
            <small>Desired speed</small>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

          <div class="flow-block">
            <span>2</span>
            <strong>Error</strong>
            <small>Difference amplifier</small>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

          <div class="flow-block">
            <span>3</span>
            <strong>Controller</strong>
            <small>I-compensator</small>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

          <div class="flow-block">
            <span>4</span>
            <strong>Motor</strong>
            <small>Physical system</small>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

          <div class="flow-block">
            <span>5</span>
            <strong>Speed Sensor</strong>
            <small>Feedback voltage</small>
          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- MOTOR CHARACTERIZATION -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">Motor Characterization</p>

        <h2>
          Measuring the physical system before designing the controller.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Rather than relying on nominal motor specifications, the motors were
          characterized experimentally. A variable motor supply allowed voltage,
          current, encoder frequency, and motor speed to be measured over a
          range of operating conditions.
        </p>


        <!-- MOTOR EQUIVALENT CIRCUIT -->
        <div class="figure-block">

          <div class="motor-model-image">
            <img
              src="/projects/motor-control/motor-equivalent-circuit.png"
              alt="Electrical and mechanical equivalent circuits of the DC motor"
            />
          </div>

          <p class="figure-caption">
            Electrical and mechanical equivalent circuits used to model the DC motor
            and relate voltage, current, torque, and angular velocity.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- PARAMETER CARDS -->
        <div class="parameter-grid">

          <article class="parameter-card">

            <span class="parameter-symbol">R<sub>M</sub></span>

            <strong>2.33 Ω</strong>

            <h3>Armature Resistance</h3>

            <p>
              The rotor was physically stalled so angular velocity and back-EMF
              were zero. Motor voltage was plotted against measured current, and
              the slope of the resulting relationship was used to determine the
              armature resistance.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="parameter-card">

            <span class="parameter-symbol">k</span>

            <strong>0.387</strong>

            <h3>Motor Constant</h3>

            <p>
              The unloaded motor was operated at multiple steady-state speeds.
              Encoder frequency was converted to angular velocity and combined
              with voltage and current measurements to determine the motor
              constant from back-EMF.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="parameter-card">

            <span class="parameter-symbol">B</span>

            <strong>0.0119</strong>

            <h3>Viscous Friction</h3>

            <p>
              Motor torque was calculated from measured current and plotted
              against angular velocity. The slope of the fitted relationship
              provided the viscous-friction coefficient.
            </p>

          </article>


          <article class="parameter-card">

            <span class="parameter-symbol">T<sub>INT</sub></span>

            <strong>0.0435 N·m</strong>

            <h3>Internal Friction Torque</h3>

            <p>
              The y-intercept of the torque-versus-speed relationship was used
              to estimate the internal friction torque of the motor.
            </p>

          </article>

        </div>


        <!-- CHARACTERIZATION GRAPH 1 -->
        <div class="figure-block">

          <div class="characterization-graph">
            <img
              src="/projects/motor-control/rm-characterization.png"
              alt="Measured motor voltage versus current used to determine armature resistance"
            />
          </div>

          <p class="figure-caption">
            Measured motor voltage versus current with linear fits used to
            determine armature resistance R<sub>M</sub>.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- CHARACTERIZATION GRAPH 2 -->
        <div class="figure-block">

          <div class="characterization-graph">
            <img
              src="/projects/motor-control/torque-speed-characterization.png"
              alt="Motor torque versus angular velocity characterization graph"
            />
          </div>

          <p class="figure-caption">
            Torque versus angular velocity used to determine viscous friction
            and internal friction torque.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- MODEL VALIDATION -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">Motor Model</p>

        <h2>
          Turning experimental measurements into a validated dynamic model.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The measured electrical and mechanical parameters were incorporated
          into a SIMetrix motor model. Experimental test conditions were then
          recreated in simulation to check whether the modeled motor behaved
          like the physical motor.
        </p>

        <p>
          Model validation compared the simulated motor spin-down response with
          the measured response of the physical motor. The simulated spin-down
          time was 1.07 seconds compared with a measured spin-down time of
          0.936 seconds, providing a close comparison between the model and the
          physical system.
        </p>


        <div class="validation-results">

          <article>
            <span>Simulated</span>
            <strong>1.07 s</strong>
            <p>Spin-down time</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Measured</span>
            <strong>0.936 s</strong>
            <p>Spin-down time</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Validated Inertia</span>
            <strong>0.00989 kg·m²</strong>
            <p>J</p>
          </article>

        </div>


        <!-- SIMULATION VS MEASUREMENT -->
        <div class="two-image-grid">

          <div>

            <div class="validation-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/motor-model-simulation.png"
                alt="SIMetrix simulation of DC motor spin-down response"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Simulated motor turn-off response. Motor voltage and angular
              velocity decay after the motor is open-circuited. Spin down time measured at 1.07s.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div>

            <div class="validation-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/motor-model-measured.png"
                alt="Oscilloscope measurement of physical DC motor spin-down response"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Measured turn-off response of the physical motor used to compare
              the experimental system with the simulated model. Spin down time measured at 0.936s.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


       <!-- SPEED SENSOR -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">Speed Sensor</p>

        <h2>
          Converting encoder pulses into an analog feedback voltage.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The motor encoder produced a pulse train whose frequency increased
          with motor speed. Because the feedback controller operated using
          analog voltages, a speed-sensor circuit was developed to convert
          encoder frequency into a proportional DC voltage.
        </p>

        <p>
          A 555-timer-based pulse circuit generated controlled output pulses,
          and a low-pass filter averaged those pulses into a smooth voltage
          representing motor speed.
        </p>


        <div class="sensor-results">

          <article>
            <span>Full-Speed Output</span>
            <strong>≈ 6.12 V</strong>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Measured Ripple</span>
            <strong>116 mV</strong>
          </article>

        </div>


        <div class="two-image-grid">

          <div>

            <div class="physical-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/speed-sensor-photo.png"
                alt="Physical encoder speed sensor circuit"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Physical encoder speed-sensor circuit.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div>

            <div class="schematic-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/speed-sensor-schematic.png"
                alt="555 timer and low-pass filter speed sensor schematic"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              555-timer and low-pass-filter speed sensor.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- SPEED SENSOR VALIDATION -->
        <div class="figure-block">

          <div class="speed-sensor-validation">
            <img
              src="/projects/motor-control/speed-sensor-validation.png"
              alt="Measured and simulated speed sensor output versus motor angular velocity"
            />
          </div>

          <p class="figure-caption">
            Speed-sensor validation comparing measured and simulated analog
            output voltage as a function of motor angular velocity.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- CONTROLLER DESIGN -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">Controller Design</p>

        <h2>
          Designing the feedback loop from the measured motor dynamics.
        </h2>

        <p>
          A difference-amplifier stage compared the reference voltage with the
          measured speed voltage to generate an error signal. That error was
          passed into an op-amp integrating amplifier, creating an
          I-compensator that adjusted the motor command until measured speed
          matched the reference.
        </p>

        <p>
          The compensator was designed analytically using the characterized
          motor model. Rather than tuning the system experimentally until it
          looked acceptable, the component values were selected to target a
          damping ratio of ζ = 1.
        </p>


        <!-- CONTROLLER DESIGN VALUES -->
        <div class="controller-values">

          <article>
            <span>Design Target</span>
            <strong>ζ = 1</strong>
            <p>Critically damped</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>Integrator Time Constant</span>
            <strong>0.171 s</strong>
            <p>R<sub>I</sub>C<sub>I</sub></p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>C<sub>I</sub></span>
            <strong>5 µF</strong>
            <p>Selected capacitor</p>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span>R<sub>I</sub></span>
            <strong>34 kΩ</strong>
            <p>Implemented resistor</p>
          </article>

        </div>


        <div class="two-image-grid">

          <div>

            <div class="schematic-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/controller-schematic.png"
                alt="Difference amplifier and I-compensator feedback controller schematic"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Difference amplifier and integrating controller used to close the
              speed-feedback loop.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div>

            <div class="physical-image">
              <img
                src="/projects/motor-control/controller-photo.png"
                alt="Physical analog motor controller breadboard"
              />
            </div>

            <p class="figure-caption">
              Physical implementation of the analog controller.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- FINAL SYSTEM -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">System Integration</p>

        <h2>
          Combining the motor, sensor, controller, and motor driver.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The characterized motor, encoder speed sensor, analog compensator,
          reference input, and motor-driver circuitry were integrated into the
          complete closed-loop system.
        </p>

        <p>
          Motor-direction control was also implemented using H-bridge
          circuitry, allowing the motor drive to be controlled independently
          from the speed-feedback loop.
        </p>


        <!-- LARGE PHYSICAL SYSTEM PHOTO -->
       <div class="system-schematic-image">
  <img
    src="/projects/motor-control/closed-loop-system-schematic.png"
    alt="Complete closed-loop motor control system schematic including motor direction control, motor driver, motor, encoder, tachometer, and I-compensator"
  />
</div>

<p class="figure-caption">
  Complete closed-loop system showing the motor-direction and drive circuitry,
  motor model, encoder, speed sensor, and I-compensator feedback loop.
</p>

      </section>


      <!-- TESTING -->
      <section class="content-section result-section">

        <p class="section-label">Testing & Final Result</p>

        <h2>
          Measured critically damped motor-speed response.
        </h2>

        <p>
          The completed feedback loop was tested using a changing reference
          voltage. A rectangular reference waveform commanded the motor between
          two operating speeds while the encoder-based sensor measured the
          resulting response.
        </p>

        <p>
          The measured motor speed transitioned quickly between commanded
          values without visible oscillation, demonstrating an approximately
          critically damped response consistent with the ζ = 1 controller
          design.
        </p>


        <!-- FINAL OSCILLOSCOPE SCREENSHOT -->
        <div class="scope-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/motor-control/closed-loop-response.png"
            alt="Oscilloscope capture showing critically damped closed-loop motor speed response"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="scope-caption">
          Dynamic closed-loop test. Reference voltage commanded a change in
          motor speed while the speed-sensor output tracked the response.
        </p>


        <div class="final-result">

          <span>Final Result</span>

          <strong>
            Stable speed tracking with short rise and fall times and no visible
            oscillation.
          </strong>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- WHAT I LEARNED -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <p class="section-label">What I Learned</p>

        <h2>
          Connecting physical measurements, mathematical models, and working
          hardware.
        </h2>

        <p>
          This project demonstrated how experimental system characterization
          can directly inform controller design. Instead of treating the motor
          as an ideal component, I measured its physical parameters, validated
          a dynamic model, designed the feedback controller from that model,
          and verified the predicted response experimentally.
        </p>


        <div class="skills">

          <span>Feedback Control</span>
          <span>Analog Circuits</span>
          <span>System Identification</span>
          <span>DC Motor Modeling</span>
          <span>Op-Amps</span>
          <span>Encoder Sensing</span>
          <span>555 Timers</span>
          <span>SIMetrix</span>
          <span>MATLAB</span>
          <span>Oscilloscope Testing</span>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- BOTTOM NAV -->
      <div class="bottom-nav">
        <a href="/#projects">← Back to all projects</a>
      </div>

